# TODO - Minecraft Java Edition

## Objective

Independently reconstruct the latest stable Java Edition client, server
and launcher in Rust. Preserve the familiar official launcher workflow
without impersonating Microsoft or Mojang. Maintain broad historical
version, world and Java mod compatibility as an explicit long-term goal.

## Acceptance

- Target the latest stable Java Edition at implementation time, with
  one current native Rust gameplay runtime, not separate native forks
  for every historical release.
- Implement a Rust launcher with familiar account, installation, version,
  mod profile, settings and launch workflows. Require legitimate Microsoft
  authentication and verified Java Edition entitlement before play.
  Use authorized account flows; never collect passwords, bypass ownership
  checks, forge sessions or embed private credentials.
- Support historical official version installations and their appropriate
  Java runtimes and loaders through isolated launcher profiles. Distinguish
  original Java execution from native Rust execution in all compatibility
  reports. Preserve existing saves and instance data on updates.
- Aim for compatibility with unmodified Java mods on the native runtime.
  Evaluate a JVM bridge, bytecode execution, versioned class and API
  adapters, event hooks, mappings and loader contracts as needed.
  Preserve a Rust core; use an appropriate Java runtime for Java mods
  rather than assuming a Rust rewrite can execute arbitrary JARs.
- Test representative Fabric, Forge, NeoForge and historical loader
  combinations, including mixins, reflection, dependencies and networking.
  Maintain a per-version and per-loader compatibility matrix. Record
  incompatible mods explicitly; do not claim universal compatibility.
- Preserve supported historical world formats and protocol interoperability;
  test migrations on copies, never overwrite worlds destructively.
- Reconstruct survival, creative, crafting, redstone, entities, world
  generation, lighting, chunk streaming and client/server simulation.
- Extract reusable voxel, spatial, rendering, construction, scripting,
  persistence and network mechanisms only where justified by real needs.
  Reuse existing project systems and verify previous title regressions.
- Treat mods as executable untrusted code. Isolate execution where feasible,
  bound resource use, protect credentials and respect third-party licences.
- Verify performance, observable behaviour and supported platforms.
  Do not distribute proprietary game binaries, assets or modified official
  distributions. Respect official terms and branding requirements.
- Check authorized local input in `.reference/games/minecraft/`.

### Optional experiment - edition and version IR

- Evaluate a unified native Rust runtime combining independently
  implemented Java and Bedrock Edition capabilities where compatible.
  Preserve edition-specific rules through explicit profiles rather than
  silently changing either edition's behaviour.
- Prototype a version/edition IR for world schemas, block and entity
  identities, gameplay rules, network protocols, registries and mod APIs.
  Aim to support any historical or future version through admitted
  adapters; compatibility must be demonstrated for each version, not
  inferred from the existence of the IR.
- Evaluate cross-edition interoperability, lossless world migration,
  version-specific mechanics and legitimate edition entitlements.
  Keep separate version adapters where semantics cannot be reconciled.
- This is a discardable research hypothesis, not a mandatory acceptance
  criterion. Compare a minimal prototype against the Java-first approach.
  Simplify, defer or abandon the unified IR and Bedrock integration if
  complexity, licensing, performance or fidelity make it impractical.
  Do not block the baseline Java implementation or the preceding catalogue.

